# Trivy ignore file
# Docs: https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#by-finding-ids
#
# One finding ID per line. Supported IDs include CVEs, GHSAs, secret rule IDs,
# misconfiguration IDs, and license findings. Blank lines and `#` comments are
# allowed. An optional `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` suffix lets an entry auto-expire.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHEN TO ADD AN ENTRY HERE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only suppress individual findings that are genuinely NOT APPLICABLE to this
# project — e.g. a CVE in a code path that is not reachable, or a false-positive
# keyword match. Every entry MUST be accompanied by a comment that records:
#   - why the finding does not apply,
#   - who decided it (ticket/PR reference),
#   - an expiry date so the assumption gets re-validated.
#
# Do NOT use this file to hide unpatched vulnerabilities you simply do not
# want to fix today — that defeats the purpose of a scanner.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHEN TO USE SOMETHING ELSE INSTEAD
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-finding suppressions quickly become unmaintainable for a whole image
# whose CVEs churn upstream (e.g. the local-only Keycloak container used for
# the docker-compose demo). For that case, exclude the PATH rather than list
# every CVE — see `trivy.yaml` (`scan.skip-dirs`) in this repo.
#
# For Java dependency findings coming out of `mvn verify` (OWASP Dependency-
# Check), use `dependency-check-suppressions.xml` instead; that is a separate
# scanner with its own suppression format.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXAMPLE (keep commented-out, this repo currently suppresses nothing)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2024-12345 exp:2026-12-31  # not reachable: we never deserialize user input, see PR #42
